Bio Computing and Data Research - Esam Aziez

Forward
Biocomputing is now at the intersection of computer science, biology, and data engineering.
Because traditional silicon based storage is starting to reach its maximum capacity, both
when it comes to energy and stored data density, synthetic biology has started to offer a
workaround through the use of cellular/molecular biology combined with tech componenets.
As of right now, since this technology is rather new, scientists have currently started
using it to process various different types of information.

Core Concepts In This New Technology
Recent studies demonstrate that engineered bacteria can implement logical computations using 
spatial diffusion and gene circuits, creating new and upcoming digital processing behavior inside 
biological media (Fedorec et al., 2024). This approach turns cellular structures into decentralized 
micro-processors capable of handling complex biological and computational data simultaneously, 
minimizing traditional silicon hardware needed whilst operating freely inside wetware environments.

Synthetic neuromorphic computing inside living cells has started bridging traditional artificial 
neural network architectures with organic wetware, allowing for adaptive logic operations at a cellular 
level (Rizik et al., 2022). By copying biological neural pathways through molecular engineering, 
these systems can process variable data dynamically with exceptionally low power consumption compared 
to standard digital hardware, opening doors for biological sensors and organic processors.
